ChatSched — Claude fixes, Sept 4
=================================

Scope: this file documents ONLY the fourth task resolved from
"Claude To fix 1..txt" — item 8, HIGH: Business profile data leakage
through shared-request/conversation policies. Items 5, 6, and 7 were
already completed and documented separately ("claude fixes Sept 1"
through "Sept 3"). Items 9 onward were left untouched.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What the finding said, verified against the real source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two policies on public.profiles, both confirmed by reading the actual
SQL:

  profiles_select_via_shared_request (schema_phase6.sql) — lets a
  publisher read a business's profiles row if that publisher has a
  requests row from that business.

  profiles_select_via_shared_conversation (schema_phase29_conversations.sql)
  — the same idea, via the conversations table instead.

Both policies' own comments say the intent is just showing a business
name to the publisher counterpart. But RLS is a row filter, not a column
filter — once either exists() check passes, the ENTIRE profiles row
becomes readable: phone, industry, city, province, website, and every
verification flag added since (schema_phase7.sql). This is real and
independent of what any frontend file renders: a publisher who shares
one active request with a business can fetch that business's phone
number today via a direct PostgREST call, regardless of the fact that no
UI currently displays it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Why this one needed a different shape than item 6's fix
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Item 6 (public publisher columns) could simply narrow the base table's
RLS policy, because nothing legitimate depended on a non-owner reading a
full publisher row through the base table — public browsing moved
entirely to a view. This one is different: AuthContext.tsx loads the
LOGGED-IN USER'S OWN full profile via `select("*")` at session start,
used throughout the app (a business editing their own phone/city/
province in Dashboard.tsx, among other things). profiles_select_own_or_
admin has to keep granting full columns for a user's own row — that
policy is untouched here. Postgres column-level GRANTs can't be
conditioned on which RLS policy branch matched, so there's no way to
keep "authenticated" able to read their own phone via the base table
while blocking them from reading someone else's phone via that same
grant. The only way to give a genuinely narrower result for the
cross-party case specifically is a separate, column-restricted view with
its own row logic — exactly what the finding's own required fix asks
for ("explicit safe views/RPCs for cross-party profile access").

I looked for an avatar field to include, per the finding's suggested
return shape (display name / business name / avatar / permitted public
metadata) — confirmed there is no avatar concept on profiles at all in
this codebase; schema_phase34_social_connect.sql's avatar_url column is
on a completely different table, for publishers' connected social
accounts. So the safe surface here is exactly full_name and
company_name, which I confirmed by reading every real consumer of the
two policies before writing the view — none of them ever read anything
else off the embedded business object.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. What I found while tracing every real caller, beyond the finding
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Traced every place `business:profiles(...)` was embedded via PostgREST,
not just the two files the finding names (those are the schema files
that DEFINE the vulnerable policies; the actual exposure lives wherever
the frontend uses them):

  Genuinely relies on profiles_select_via_shared_request (fixed):
    - src/components/PublisherDashboardView.tsx (`requests` query)
    - src/lib/campaignWorkspace.ts (`requests` query)

  Genuinely relies on profiles_select_via_shared_conversation (fixed):
    - src/pages/Messages.tsx (`conversations` query)

  Looked like it should also be affected, but isn't — confirmed by
  checking whether any policy anywhere covers a channel_requests ->
  profiles relationship (it doesn't, none does):
    - src/components/PublisherDashboardView.tsx and
      src/lib/campaignWorkspace.ts each ALSO embed `business:profiles(...)`
      on a `channel_requests` query. No RLS policy in this entire schema
      has ever covered that relationship, so that embed was already
      silently returning null before this fix — a pre-existing,
      unrelated gap, not something this change makes worse. Left alone;
      see "what's not done" below.

  Also looked related, but a different (already-public) relationship
  entirely:
    - src/pages/MediaKit.tsx and src/pages/PublisherProfile.tsx each
      embed `business:profiles(full_name, company_name)` on a `reviews`
      query, to attribute a public review to the business that wrote it.
      reviews_select_public already makes those rows fully public
      regardless of either policy this fix touches, and neither policy's
      exists() check ever matches a reviews row in the first place — so
      this embed's behavior (already inconsistent: it only resolves a
      name today when the viewer happens to coincidentally also share an
      unrelated request/conversation with that same business) is
      completely unaffected by removing the two policies. Left alone —
      see "what's not done" below.

