5. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- A view with its own auth.uid()-aware WHERE clause, not narrower RLS
  policies on profiles itself, because (as explained above) Postgres
  column-level GRANTs can't be conditioned on which policy branch
  matched a row, and AuthContext.tsx's own-profile load genuinely needs
  every column on the own-row path. A view was the only mechanism that
  could give a real answer to "full columns for your own row, name-only
  for someone else's" at once.
- A separate top-level query (via a small shared helper) rather than
  trying to make the view embeddable under the `profiles` relationship
  name, because PostgREST's embedding depends on a real foreign-key
  constraint that a view doesn't carry, and betting a security fix on an
  embedding trick working across PostgREST versions — with no live
  instance in this sandbox to verify it — is exactly the kind of
  "assumed rather than confirmed" mistake this whole exercise is meant
  to avoid.
- Left the channel_requests-embed and reviews-embed cases alone rather
  than "fixing" them into working, because neither ever depended on the
  two policies this task removes — extending business_contact_public's
  reach to cover them would be adding a new capability under the banner
  of a security fix, not restoring something the fix broke.

