6. What's NOT done, and why that's a deliberate boundary here
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- The channel_requests -> profiles relationship (PublisherDashboardView.tsx
  and campaignWorkspace.ts each still embed `business:profiles(...)` on
  their channel_requests query, both no-ops today) is a real, separate
  gap — a publisher using a "request"-flow channel currently can't see
  the business's name on that dashboard at all. Worth fixing, but it's a
  missing feature, not a security hole, and the finding's own
  affected-files list names only schema_phase6.sql and
  schema_phase29_conversations.sql.
- The reviews -> profiles relationship (MediaKit.tsx, PublisherProfile.tsx)
  is similarly a real, separate inconsistency (a public review's author
  business name resolves inconsistently depending on the viewer's
  unrelated relationships) — also worth a dedicated look, also out of
  this task's named scope.
- Items 9 through 13 in "Claude To fix 1..txt" are untouched.
- Claude to fix 2.txt and Claude to fix 3.txt were not opened.
- Same standing honesty note this project's test suite has carried since
  it began: no Postgres, Docker, or Supabase CLI in this sandbox, so
  neither the new migration nor the new test file has been run against a
  real database. Everything here was reviewed by hand, line-by-line,
  against the actual current policy definitions and every real caller in
  the codebase — not written from memory of what they "should" say. The
  first real `supabase test db` run is the actual test of whether this
  is correct.
